+# Base USDC Yield Vault
+
+An ERC-4626 vault on Base. Depositors put in USDC; the strategy pairs it with WETH in the
+Aerodrome USDC/WETH volatile pool, stakes the LP in that pool's gauge, and a keeper calls
+`harvest()` to claim the AERO emissions and compound them back into the position.
+
+```
+depositor ──USDC──> YieldVault ──USDC──> AerodromeLpStrategy
+                    (ERC-4626)            ├─ swap ½ to WETH        (Aerodrome Router)
+                    idle buffer           ├─ addLiquidity          (vAMM-WETH/USDC)
+                                          ├─ stake LP              (Gauge → AERO)
+                                          └─ harvest: AERO → USDC → back into the LP
+                                             prices/bounds from    (Chainlink ETH-USD, USDC-USD)
+```
+
+| Contract | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `src/YieldVault.sol` | ERC-4626 over USDC. Shares, idle buffer, deposit cap, performance fee, profit unlocking, keeper roles. |
+| `src/AerodromeLpStrategy.sol` | Swaps, LPs, stakes, harvests. Owns all slippage and oracle logic. |
+| `src/libraries/VolatileLpMath.sol` | Manipulation-resistant fair pricing of `xy=k` LP tokens. |
+| `script/Deploy.s.sol` | Deploys and configures both, then hands ownership to a multisig. |
+| `script/BaseAddresses.sol` | Base mainnet addresses, each verified on-chain (see below). |
+
+---
+
+## Read this first: what depositors are actually exposed to
+
+**This is not a principal-protected USDC product.** Half of every deposit is swapped into WETH to
+form the LP. A depositor therefore holds a 50/50 USDC/WETH position and carries impermanent loss
+against ETH. In USDC terms:
+
+- ETH falls 50% → the vault's share price falls roughly 25–30%, even with every reward compounded.
+- ETH doubles → the share price rises roughly 35–45%, i.e. **less** than simply holding the pair.
+
+`test_EthDrawdownProducesImpermanentLoss` and `test_EthRallyLagsHoldingEth` pin these ranges down
+rather than leaving them implicit. If the intent was a principal-preserving USDC yield product,
+this strategy is the wrong shape and a lending market (Aave/Moonwell/Morpho on Base) or a
+stable-stable Aerodrome pool would be the right one. The task specified USDC paired with WETH in
+DEX liquidity, so that is what is built here — but the risk should be a deliberate choice, and
+the front end must state it.
+
+The second thing to know: **staking Aerodrome LP in a gauge forfeits the pool's trading fees.**
+Aerodrome redirects them to the voters who directed emissions to that pool. So the yield here is
+AERO emissions, and nothing else. That is why `harvest()` only ever handles AERO.
+
+---
+
+## Why these integrations
+
+**Aerodrome** — Aerodrome is the dominant DEX on Base by both TVL and volume, and its USDC/WETH
+volatile pool is the deepest venue for that pair on the chain. Depth is the binding constraint
+here: everything the strategy does is a swap, and swap cost scales with how thin the pool is.
+It is a Velodrome V2 fork, so the code is well-audited and long-lived, and its ve(3,3) gauge
+system gives a first-class staking primitive (`deposit`/`withdraw`/`getReward`) rather than
+requiring a bespoke farming contract.
+
+**Aerodrome gauge over plain LP** — an unstaked LP earns trading fees; a staked LP earns AERO
+emissions instead. For the USDC/WETH pool, emissions have historically been the larger of the
+two, and they arrive in a single token on a schedule the vault controls the claiming of, which is
+far easier to compound predictably than fees that accrue continuously inside the pool.
+
+**Aerodrome over Uniswap V3** — Uniswap V3 on Base is deep, but concentrated liquidity requires
+active range management. A v1 vault with a range that drifts out of band silently stops earning
+and ends up 100% in one asset. Aerodrome's `vAMM` is full-range: no rebalancing logic, no
+position NFTs, and a valuation with a closed form. That is the right complexity budget for a
+first version. If yield needs to improve later, a V3/Slipstream strategy can be added behind the
+same `IStrategy` interface without touching depositor-facing code.
+
+**Chainlink over the pool's own reserves** — this is the single most important choice in the
+codebase. Every price the strategy uses (share valuation, every swap's `minOut`, every
+`removeLiquidity` bound) comes from Chainlink, never from `getReserves()`. Spot reserves are
+attacker-controlled within a single transaction; a vault that prices its shares off them can be
+drained with a flash loan. Both legs are read — ETH/USD **and** USDC/USD — so the vault prices in
+real USDC rather than assuming USDC is worth exactly one dollar, and a depeg shows up honestly.
+
+**OpenZeppelin ERC-4626** — standard vault semantics for free, and its virtual-share offset is a
+well-understood answer to the first-depositor inflation attack. Shares use a 12-decimal offset
+(18-decimal shares over a 6-decimal asset), which makes the donation attack cost roughly a
+trillion times any gain; `test_DonationAttackOnFirstDepositorIsNotProfitable` demonstrates the
+attacker ends up down and the victim whole.
+
+### Addresses (Base, chainId 8453)
+
+Every one of these was read back from Base mainnet, not copied from documentation — token
+`symbol()`/`decimals()`, `Router.defaultFactory()`, `PoolFactory.getPool()`, `Voter.gauges()`,
+`Gauge.stakingToken()`/`rewardToken()`, and each feed's `description()`.
+
+| | Address |
+| --- | --- |
+| USDC | `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` |
+| WETH | `0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000006` |
+| AERO | `0x940181a94A35A4569E4529A3CDfB74e38FD98631` |
+| Aerodrome Router | `0xcF77a3Ba9A5CA399B7c97c74d54e5b1Beb874E43` |
+| Aerodrome PoolFactory | `0x420DD381b31aEf6683db6B902084cB0FFECe40Da` |
+| `vAMM-WETH/USDC` pool | `0xcDAC0d6c6C59727a65F871236188350531885C43` |
+| Gauge for that pool | `0x519BBD1Dd8C6A94C46080E24f316c14Ee758C025` |
+| Chainlink ETH/USD | `0x71041dddad3595F9CEd3DcCFBe3D1F4b0a16Bb70` |
+| Chainlink USDC/USD | `0x7e860098F58bBFC8648a4311b374B1D669a2bc6B` |
+
+> In that pool **token0 is WETH and token1 is USDC** — Aerodrome sorts pool tokens by address.
+> Nothing in the code assumes USDC is token0; the strategy reads the ordering at construction.
+
+The strategy's constructor re-verifies the wiring on-chain: that the gauge really stakes this
+pool, that the pool really is the USDC/WETH volatile pair, and it reads AERO off the gauge rather
+than trusting a constant. A stale address in `BaseAddresses.sol` fails the deployment instead of
+silently misrouting funds.
+
+---
+
+## Build and test
+
+```bash
+forge build
+forge test
+```
+
+72 tests across four suites: `YieldVault.t.sol` (accounting, withdrawals, access control),
+`Harvest.t.sol` (compounding, fees, profit locking, keeper guardrails), `StrategySecurity.t.sol`
+(oracles, manipulation resistance, LP math, impermanent loss), `Emergency.t.sol`.
+
+Tests run against a faithful constant-product mock — real fee-on-input, real price impact, real
+`k` invariant check — not stubs, so slippage guards are genuinely exercised. `BaseTest` also
+provides `_arbPoolToOracle()`, which stands in for the arbitrage that keeps a live pool pinned to
+the market price; the strategy deliberately declines to trade while the pool is dislocated, so any
+test that deploys capital over several calls has to model that.
+
+---
+
+## Deployment
+
+```bash
+export PRIVATE_KEY=0x...          # deployer
+export VAULT_OWNER=0x...          # multisig that ends up owning both contracts
+export TREASURY=0x...             # performance-fee recipient
+export KEEPER=0x...               # harvest()/allocate() caller
+export DEPOSIT_CAP=1000000000000  # optional, USDC base units (default 1,000,000 USDC)
+export MIN_AERO_PRICE_E6=200000   # optional, floor price for the AERO sale (default $0.20)
+
+forge script script/Deploy.s.sol:Deploy \
+  --rpc-url "$BASE_RPC_URL" --broadcast --verify
+```
+
+The script deploys the vault, deploys the strategy pointed at it, links them, registers the
+keeper, sets the deposit cap and the AERO price floor, then calls `transferOwnership` to
+`VAULT_OWNER` on both. Ownership is `Ownable2Step`, so **the multisig must call
+`acceptOwnership()` on both contracts** before the deployment is finished. Until it does, the
+deployer key still controls them.
+
+### Sizing the deposit cap
+
+The binding constraint is pool depth, not the contract. The strategy will only swap
+`maxSwapPoolShareBps` (default 0.25%) of the pool's reserve per call, so a deposit much larger
+than that deploys over several `allocate()` calls spread across blocks, waiting for arbitrage to
+reset the pool between them. Start with a cap you can deploy in a handful of calls and raise it
+as the position beds in. Oversized deposits degrade gracefully — they sit idle and fully
+accounted for, they do not revert or get stuck (`test_OversizedDepositDeploysInSlices`).
+
+### Launch checklist
+
+1. Verify both contracts on Basescan.
+2. Multisig calls `acceptOwnership()` on the vault and the strategy.
+3. Confirm `strategy.ethPriceInUsdc()` returns a sane number and `strategy.poolDeviationBps()` is
+   small.
+4. Seed a small deposit yourself and run one full `allocate()` → `harvest()` → `redeem()` cycle
+   before opening deposits.
+5. Set `MIN_AERO_PRICE_E6` to something meaningful for current AERO prices, and keep it updated.
+
+---
+
+## Keeper operation
+
+The keeper is a hot key with two jobs. It cannot move funds anywhere except into and out of the
+strategy's own position, and every path it can touch is bounded on-chain.
+
+**`vault.allocate()`** — pushes idle USDC above the target buffer (`targetIdleBps`, default 5%)
+into the strategy and deploys it. Deposits deliberately do **not** auto-allocate: swapping and
+LPing on every user deposit would be expensive and would hand every depositor's entry to
+searchers. Call it when idle exceeds the buffer by a worthwhile margin.
+
+**`vault.harvest(minAssetsFromRewards)`** — claims AERO from the gauge, sells it for USDC, takes
+the performance fee, and compounds the rest back into the LP.
+
+Sizing `minAssetsFromRewards`, off-chain:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 pending  = strategy.pendingRewards();                        // AERO claimable
+Route[] memory r = strategy.aeroSaleRoute();                         // governance-set route
+uint256[] memory amounts = router.getAmountsOut(pending, r);
+uint256 minOut   = amounts[amounts.length - 1] * 9_900 / 10_000;     // e.g. 1% tolerance
+vault.harvest(minOut);
+```
+
+Suggested cadence: harvest when `pendingRewards()` valued in USDC exceeds roughly 50–100x the gas
+cost of the transaction. On Base that is usually daily. Harvesting more often is not harmful, just
+wasteful; the AERO keeps accruing in the gauge until claimed.
+
+### Why the keeper can't rug the harvest
+
+- `minAssetsFromRewards` is floored on-chain by `minAeroPriceE6`, a governance-set price. A keeper
+  that passes `minOut = 0` — or a stolen key attempting to hand the harvest to a sandwicher — is
+  rejected (`test_KeeperCannotDumpRewardsBelowTheGovernanceFloor`). There is no Chainlink AERO
+  feed on Base, which is why this floor is a governance parameter rather than an oracle read.
+- The AERO sale **route** is set by the owner, not the keeper, so a keeper cannot route the sale
+  through a pool it controls.
+- The USDC↔WETH swaps and the `removeLiquidity` bounds are derived from Chainlink inside the
+  contract. The keeper supplies no slippage parameters for them at all.
+- `harvest()` and `allocate()` are the only keeper-callable functions. Neither can send funds to
+  an arbitrary address.
+
+### When calls become no-ops or revert
+
+- **Pool dislocated** (`poolDeviationBps() > maxPoolDeviationBps`, default 1%): the strategy
+  declines to add liquidity and returns early. `harvest()` still claims and sells the AERO; the
+  proceeds wait as idle USDC and get deployed on a later call. Nothing is lost — this is the
+  guard refusing to LP into a pool that disagrees with the oracle.
+- **Chainlink stale** (older than `maxOracleStaleness`, default 3h): every oracle-priced path
+  reverts, including deposits and withdrawals. See the break-glass path below.
+- **Nothing to do**: `harvest()` with no pending rewards returns 0 and is otherwise inert.
+
+---
+
+## Design decisions worth knowing
+
+**Fair LP valuation.** `totalAssets()` never uses spot reserves. It takes only the invariant
+`k = r₀·r₁` from the pool — which a swap cannot decrease — and reconstructs the reserves the pool
+*would* hold at the Chainlink price: `value = 2·√(k·P)`. A manipulator can raise the reported
+value only by donating fees into `k`. `test_ShareValuationIgnoresPoolManipulation` pushes 10m USDC
+through the pool, moving spot by a third, and the share price moves by under 0.2%.
+
+**Profit unlocking.** `harvest()` is the moment reward value is realised. If it landed instantly,
+anyone could deposit in the block before a harvest and redeem in the block after for a risk-free
+cut. Harvested profit is added to `lockedProfit` and released linearly over `profitUnlockPeriod`
+(default 6h), so the share price ramps instead of jumping.
+`test_SandwichingAHarvestIsNotProfitable` shows a 500k attacker doing exactly this and losing
+money. Back-to-back harvests carry the unreleased remainder forward rather than flushing it.
+
+**Exit costs land on the exiting user.** Unwinding an LP position costs swap fees and price impact
+that `totalAssets()` cannot know in advance. `redeem()` therefore tolerates a bounded shortfall
+(`defaultMaxLossBps`, default 0.5%) and charges it to the redeemer rather than socialising it
+across remaining holders; `redeem(shares, receiver, owner, maxLossBps)` lets a caller set their
+own tolerance. `withdraw()`, where the caller names an exact asset amount, reverts instead if it
+cannot be filled exactly.
+
+**Unclaimed AERO is excluded from `totalAssets()`.** Pricing it would need an AERO oracle that does
+not exist on Base. Excluding it understates AUM slightly between harvests, which is the safe
+direction — it can never inflate the share price — and profit unlocking already smooths the step.
+
+**Slippage budget.** `maxSwapSlippageBps` (1.5%) has to cover three things at once: how far the
+pool may sit from the oracle (`maxPoolDeviationBps`, 1%), the pool's own fee, and the price impact
+of our trade (bounded by `maxSwapPoolShareBps`, 0.25%). Change one and re-check the sum, or
+invests will start reverting. Every knob is capped at 10% in its setter, so governance cannot
+widen them far enough to let a keeper drain the position through swaps.
+
+---
+
+## Emergency procedures
+
+**Normal unwind** — `vault.emergencyExit()` (owner). Unstakes, removes liquidity, sells the WETH
+leg, returns everything to the vault, pauses deposits, and latches the strategy into
+`emergencyMode` so a routine `allocate()` cannot walk the funds back in. Withdrawals stay open
+throughout.
+
+**Chainlink is dead** — `strategy.emergencyExitManual(minUsdcFromLp, minWethFromLp, minUsdcFromSwap)`
+(owner). Every other path prices its bounds off Chainlink, so a feed that stops updating would
+otherwise freeze the position *including the normal emergency exit*. This path reads no oracle;
+governance supplies the three bounds by hand. Once the position is fully in USDC, `totalAssets()`
+stops touching the oracle too, so users can withdraw again
+(`test_ManualExitWorksWithADeadOracle`).
+
+**Re-arming** — `strategy.setEmergencyMode(false)` then `vault.setDepositsPaused(false)`.
+
+---
+
+## Known limitations
+
+These are deliberate v1 scope decisions, not oversights.
+
+1. **Impermanent loss against ETH** is the dominant risk and is not hedged. See the top of this
+   file.
+2. **Single strategy, single pool.** No allocation across venues. `IStrategy` is the seam where
+   that would go.
+3. **Trusted governance.** The owner can change slippage parameters (within the 10% caps), the
+   AERO sale route, and the fee (capped at 20%), and can trigger an exit. It should be a multisig
+   with a timelock; this version ships neither.
+4. **The AERO price floor is a manual parameter.** With no Chainlink AERO feed on Base, keeping
+   `minAeroPriceE6` current is an operational responsibility. Set too high, harvests revert; set
+   too low, it stops being a meaningful guard.
+5. **Oracle liveness is a hard dependency.** A stale feed halts deposits and withdrawals until it
+   recovers or governance runs the manual exit.
+6. **Deposit size is bounded by pool depth**, not by the contract. See "Sizing the deposit cap".
+7. **Not audited.** The mock AMM makes the tests meaningful but is not the real Aerodrome
+   implementation; fork tests against Base mainnet should come before any real deployment, and
+   then an audit.
